\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Transposing a field operator and its links}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a finite periodic grid #{\Lambda} with unit steps #{e_j},
take a spin module #{S} with a
\vocabk{charge conjugation of sign}{fdip-017H} #{c_\eta}.
Let #{F} be a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian fiber with
unitary represented links #{U_j(x)}. For its conjugate space
#{\bar F}, let #{c_F:F\to\bar F} be the canonical antiunitary
#{v\mapsto\bar v}. The links
#{\bar U_j(x)=c_F U_j(x)c_F^{-1}} act on #{\bar F}.
On fields with values in #{S\otimes F}, apply
#{c_\eta\otimes c_F} at each site to obtain the antiunitary
##{\mathcal Q:\ell^2(\Lambda;S\otimes F)
\longrightarrow \ell^2(\Lambda;S\otimes\bar F).}}

\p{Take spacing #{a>0} and use the
\vocabk{covariant lattice Dirac operator}{fdip-009O} and
\vocabk{Wilson operator}{fdip-009U} with these directions,
with #{-L_{a,U}\ge0}. For adjoints in the field inner product,
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Q D_{a,U}^{\dagger}\mathcal Q^{-1}
  &=-\eta D_{a,\bar U},\\
\mathcal Q L_{a,U}^{\dagger}\mathcal Q^{-1}
  &=L_{a,\bar U}.
\end{aligned}}
For #{\eta=-1} and the same real Wilson coefficient #{c_W},
##{\mathcal Q D_{W,a,U}^{\dagger}\mathcal Q^{-1}
=D_{W,a,\bar U}.}
These identities act on the field space with fiber #{\bar F}.}

\proof{
\p{Moving a scalar between the spin and fiber factors conjugates
it on either one. Thus the stated action on elementary tensors
is well defined and antiunitary for the tensor inner product.
The forward shift at #{x} uses #{U_j(x)^{-1}} on the value at
#{x+e_j}. Its inverse uses #{U_j(x-e_j)} on the value at
#{x-e_j}. Conjugation by #{c_F} carries these maps to the
inverse and the direct transport for #{\bar U} at the respective
sites. Each centered difference is a real multiple of the
difference of a shift and its inverse. The Laplacian uses their
sum with real coefficients. Using the generator sign on spin gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Q D_{a,U}\mathcal Q^{-1}&=\eta D_{a,\bar U},\\
\mathcal Q L_{a,U}\mathcal Q^{-1}&=L_{a,\bar U}.
\end{aligned}}}

\p{The centered differences are skew-adjoint and commute with
the self-adjoint spin directions. Their weighted sum #{D_{a,U}}
is skew-adjoint as in \ref{fdip-009P}. The same unitary shifts
give #{L_{a,U}^\dagger=L_{a,U}} by the
[Wilson formula](fdip-009U). This proves the two adjoint
identities. When #{\eta=-1} both terms of
#{D_{a,U}-c_W L_{a,U}} have positive sign under this operation,
giving the Wilson identity.}

\p{Choose the site basis and orthonormal bases on #{S} and #{F}.
Use the same spin basis in the target and the #{c_F}-images
of the fiber basis there. The target link entries are then the
complex conjugates of the source entries. Write
#{c_\eta=V_\eta\kappa_0}, where #{\kappa_0} conjugates spin
coordinates. Extending the unitary matrix #{V_\eta} by identity
on site and fiber indices, #{\mathcal Q} acts on field coordinates
by full coordinate conjugation followed by #{V_\eta}. Conjugating
the entries of a full adjoint gives its transpose on all site,
spin and fiber indices. In particular
#{D_{a,U}^{T}=-\bar{D_{a,U}}} and
#{L_{a,U}^{T}=\bar{L_{a,U}}}. The adjoint identities read
##{\begin{aligned}
 V_\eta D_{a,U}^{T}V_\eta^{-1}&=-\eta D_{a,\bar U},\\
 V_\eta L_{a,U}^{T}V_\eta^{-1}&=L_{a,\bar U}.
\end{aligned}}
At #{\eta=-1} the same coefficient gives
##{V_\eta D_{W,a,U}^{T}V_\eta^{-1}=D_{W,a,\bar U}.}
On the real product spin space, #{V_-=Z^{\otimes n}}
by \ref{fdip-013T}.}

\p{A supplied antiunitary from #{F} to itself can replace the
canonical map to #{\bar F}. Its unitary matrix factor then
accompanies #{V_\eta} in the transpose comparison in a common
fiber basis. If it normalizes the represented group, the
transported links belong to that same group. A relation with
the original connection requires equality of the link data
or an intertwining identification with that connection.}
}
